\PYGZbs{}textbf\PYGZob{}Step 2:\PYGZcb{}

Calculate Ridge Estimate

Calculate \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}boldsymbol\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZcb{}\PYGZob{}Ridge\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{}) by minimizing the Ridge cost
function
\PYGZbs{}(C\PYGZob{}Ridge\PYGZcb{}(\PYGZbs{}boldsymbol\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZcb{}) = C\PYG{g+ge}{\PYGZus{}\PYGZob{}OLS\PYGZcb{}(\PYGZbs{}boldsymbol\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZcb{}) + \PYGZbs{}lambda \PYGZbs{}sum\PYGZus{}}\PYGZob{}k=1\PYGZcb{}\PYGZca{}\PYGZob{}10\PYGZcb{} \PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZus{}k\PYGZca{}2\PYGZbs{})

Ridge regularization adds a penalty term based on the \PYGZbs{}(L\PYGZus{}2\PYGZbs{}) norm (the
sum of the squares of the parameters) to the OLS cost function. The
regularized cost function is minimized:
\PYGZbs{}[ C\PYG{g+ge}{\PYGZus{}\PYGZob{}ridge\PYGZcb{}(\PYGZbs{}boldsymbol\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZcb{}) = \PYGZbs{}sum\PYGZus{}}\PYGZob{}i=1\PYGZcb{}\PYGZca{}\PYGZob{}N\PYGZcb{} (y\PYGZus{}i \PYGZhy{} \PYGZbs{}hat\PYGZob{}y\PYGZcb{}i)\PYGZca{}2 + \PYGZbs{}lambda \PYGZbs{}sum\PYGZob{}k=1\PYGZcb{}\PYGZca{}\PYGZob{}10\PYGZcb{} \PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZus{}k\PYGZca{}2 \PYGZbs{}]
(We assume the standard convention where the intercept \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZus{}0\PYGZbs{}) is
generally not penalized.)

\PYGZbs{}leavevmode\PYGZbs{}vadjust pre\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}hypertarget\PYGZob{}67b7e519\PYGZcb{}\PYGZob{}\PYGZcb{}\PYGZcb{}\PYGZpc{}
\PYGZbs{}textbf\PYGZob{}Step 3:\PYGZcb{}

Specify Penalty Weight (\PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda\PYGZbs{}))

Choose and explicitly state \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda\PYGZbs{}). Justify the choice (e.g.,
related to preventing overfitting)

The parameter \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda\PYGZbs{}) (denoted as alpha in
scikit\PYGZhy{}learn\PYGZbs{}textquotesingle s Ridge function) determines the weight
given to the penalty. • If \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda\PYGZbs{}) is small (\PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda \PYGZbs{}approx 0\PYGZbs{})),
the solution approaches the pure OLS solution. • If \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda\PYGZbs{}) is
large, the parameter coefficients are heavily shrunk toward zero. Since
the function values (\PYGZbs{}(Y\PYGZbs{})) are extremely large (up to \PYGZbs{}(10\PYGZca{}\PYGZob{}15\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{})), the
scale of the error term \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}sum (y\PYGZus{}i \PYGZhy{} \PYGZbs{}hat\PYGZob{}y\PYGZcb{}\PYGZus{}i)\PYGZca{}2\PYGZbs{}) is also very large.
We choose a large, illustrative \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda\PYGZbs{}) (e.g., \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda=10\PYGZca{}\PYGZob{}15\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{}))
to demonstrate a significant regularization effect, balancing the
penalty term against the sum of squared residuals.

