\PYGZbs{}textbf\PYGZob{}Step 4:\PYGZcb{}

Discuss Solution Qualities

Discuss qualities of \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}boldsymbol\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZcb{}\PYGZob{}OLS\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{}) and
\PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}boldsymbol\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZcb{}\PYGZob{}Ridge\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{}) solutions. Discuss OLS overfitting vs.
Ridge shrinkage/robustness.

The comparison focuses on the core qualities of the estimators:

\PYGZbs{}begin\PYGZob{}enumerate\PYGZcb{}
\PYGZbs{}item
  OLS: Finds the minimum of the squared error distance function.
\PYGZbs{}item
  Ridge Regression: Minimizes the squared error distance plus the
  \PYGZbs{}(L\PYG{g+ge}{\PYGZus{}2\PYGZbs{}) penalty term (\PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda \PYGZbs{}sum \PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZus{}}k\PYGZca{}2\PYGZbs{})).
\PYGZbs{}end\PYGZob{}enumerate\PYGZcb{}

\PYGZbs{}begin\PYGZob{}longtable\PYGZcb{}[]\PYGZob{}@\PYGZob{}\PYGZcb{}
  \PYGZgt{}\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}raggedright\PYGZbs{}arraybackslash\PYGZcb{}p\PYGZob{}(\PYGZbs{}columnwidth \PYGZhy{} 4\PYGZbs{}tabcolsep) * \PYGZbs{}real\PYGZob{}0.3333\PYGZcb{}\PYGZcb{}
  \PYGZgt{}\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}raggedright\PYGZbs{}arraybackslash\PYGZcb{}p\PYGZob{}(\PYGZbs{}columnwidth \PYGZhy{} 4\PYGZbs{}tabcolsep) * \PYGZbs{}real\PYGZob{}0.3333\PYGZcb{}\PYGZcb{}
  \PYGZgt{}\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}raggedright\PYGZbs{}arraybackslash\PYGZcb{}p\PYGZob{}(\PYGZbs{}columnwidth \PYGZhy{} 4\PYGZbs{}tabcolsep) * \PYGZbs{}real\PYGZob{}0.3333\PYGZcb{}\PYGZcb{}@\PYGZob{}\PYGZcb{}\PYGZcb{}
\PYGZbs{}toprule()
\PYGZbs{}begin\PYGZob{}minipage\PYGZcb{}[b]\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}linewidth\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{}raggedright
Quality
\PYGZbs{}end\PYGZob{}minipage\PYGZcb{} \PYGZam{} \PYGZbs{}begin\PYGZob{}minipage\PYGZcb{}[b]\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}linewidth\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{}raggedright
OLS Solution (No Regularization)
\PYGZbs{}end\PYGZob{}minipage\PYGZcb{} \PYGZam{} \PYGZbs{}begin\PYGZob{}minipage\PYGZcb{}[b]\PYGZob{}\PYGZbs{}linewidth\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{}raggedright
Ridge Solution (Regularization \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda=10\PYGZca{}\PYGZob{}15\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{}))
\PYGZbs{}end\PYGZob{}minipage\PYGZcb{} \PYGZbs{}\PYGZbs{}
\PYGZbs{}midrule()
\PYGZbs{}endhead
Bias/Variance \PYGZam{} Low bias, high variance. Prone to overfitting. \PYGZam{}
Introduces bias but reduces variance. Promotes a smoother fit and
prevents overfitting. \PYGZbs{}\PYGZbs{}
Parameter Magnitudes \PYGZam{} Coefficients (\PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZus{}i\PYGZbs{})) often have very large
magnitudes (especially for high degrees) that try to fit every data
point perfectly. \PYGZam{} Coefficients are shrunk significantly toward zero by
the \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda\PYGZbs{}) penalty. \PYGZbs{}\PYGZbs{}
Model Complexity \PYGZam{} High complexity; uses all 11 parameters aggressively.
\PYGZam{} Lower complexity due to coefficient shrinkage. \PYGZbs{}\PYGZbs{}
\PYGZbs{}bottomrule()
\PYGZbs{}end\PYGZob{}longtable\PYGZcb{}

Observed Quality Differences (Based on Calculations):

\PYGZbs{}begin\PYGZob{}enumerate\PYGZcb{}
\PYGZbs{}item
  OLS Coefficients: The OLS coefficients are widely varying and
  extremely large, especially for the constant term and the higher
  powers (e.g., \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZus{}0 \PYGZbs{}approx \PYGZhy{}2.59 \PYGZbs{}times 10\PYGZca{}\PYGZob{}14\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{}) and
  \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZus{}\PYGZob{}10\PYGZcb{} \PYGZbs{}approx 6.27 \PYGZbs{}times 10\PYGZca{}3\PYGZbs{})). This is the textbook
  signature of a high\PYGZhy{}degree polynomial overfitting small data,
  resulting in a model that is likely extremely \PYGZdq{}wiggly\PYGZdq{} and unstable
  (high variance).
\PYGZbs{}item
  Ridge Coefficients: The Ridge coefficients, penalized by the chosen
  large \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda\PYGZbs{}), are dramatically reduced in magnitude (e.g.,
  \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZus{}0 \PYGZbs{}approx \PYGZhy{}1.33 \PYGZbs{}times 10\PYGZca{}\PYGZob{}12\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{}) and
  \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}alpha\PYGZus{}\PYGZob{}10\PYGZcb{} \PYGZbs{}approx 6.00 \PYGZbs{}times 10\PYGZca{}3\PYGZbs{})). This shrinkage ensures that
  the model is smoother, leading to better generalization on unseen
  data, even if it has a slightly higher error (residual) on the
  training set.
\PYGZbs{}end\PYGZob{}enumerate\PYGZcb{}

Weight of the Penalty (\PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda\PYGZbs{})): The weight \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda = 10\PYGZca{}\PYGZob{}15\PYGZcb{}\PYGZbs{})
ensures the model is dominated by the regularization constraint, forcing
the parameter magnitudes to remain relatively small compared to the
scale of the raw OLS solution. The optimal \PYGZbs{}(\PYGZbs{}lambda\PYGZbs{}) would typically
be determined through cross\PYGZhy{}validation, selecting the value that
provides the best trade\PYGZhy{}off between minimizing the residual error and
minimizing the penalty term.

